Unlike chatbots [7, 44] or image generators [37], physical AI systems [3, 15, 2, 39, 28, 1, 34, 47] act while the world keeps evolving: the robot cannot afford to pause while thinking.
This reveals a structural limitation of synchronous execution with action-chunking policies [10, 18]—every inference call forces a mandatory pause between chunks during which the robot is idle but the world is not, which is fatal for dynamic tasks such as tracking moving targets [43].
Therefore, asynchronous execution—thinking while acting over action chunks—is not a latency optimization but a structural requirement.

However, naive async strategies, whether switching chunks immediately or smoothing them via temporal ensembling [49], introduce inter-chunk discontinuity or invalid actions because each new chunk is generated in isolation from the committed actions.
Real-time chunking (RTC) [4] resolves this by recasting chunk transitions as an inpainting problem: freezing committed actions from the previous chunk and consistently generating the remainder, representing a simple yet effective approach that has achieved great success in precise and smooth real-world manipulation [14, 13].

Specifically, RTC is specially designed for flow-matching policies [20].
As the predominant action head architecture in today’s state-of-the-art VLAs [3, 15, 2], flow-matching has driven much of the recent progress in large-scale robot learning.
However, in this paper, we first systematically show that RTC with a flow-matching head is far from ideal with four critical limitations:

Pre-training without Inpainting. Flow-matching policies are not pre-trained on action chunks with inconsistent noise levels for inference-time inpainting.
Therefore, scaling pre-training does not directly improve asynchronous performance, and inference-time corrections like Π\PiGDM [32] are inevitable for effective inpainting;

Pre-training without Inpainting. Flow-matching policies are not pre-trained on action chunks with inconsistent noise levels for inference-time inpainting.
Therefore, scaling pre-training does not directly improve asynchronous performance, and inference-time corrections like Π\PiGDM [32] are inevitable for effective inpainting;

Fine-tuning Required. As a consequence, adequate inpainting quality demands a dedicated fine-tuning stage with techniques such as action-suffix conditioning [5, 38] to explicitly introduce the inpainting-specific noise pattern into training;

Heuristic Guidance. Moreover, to better leverage the previous action chunks, Π\PiGDM requires a heuristic schedule fixed across different inference-time cases;

Extra Inference Cost. Finally, the correction guidance term at every denoising step roughly doubles inference cost, ironically increasing the very latency RTC aims to hide.

Many ongoing efforts [23, 25, 5, 45] seek to resolve these aforementioned issues within the flow-matching paradigm.
Instead, our key insight and observation are that by replacing the action head with a discrete diffusion policy [19], all the aforementioned limitations can be resolved at once. Or, to put it simply: Discrete Diffusion Policies are Natural Asynchronous Executors:

Inpainting as Pre-training. Discrete diffusion policies are pre-trained to inpaint upon randomly masked sequences. Therefore, scaling pre-training directly improves asynchronous performance, and the native forward pass suits inference-time inpainting;

Inpainting as Pre-training. Discrete diffusion policies are pre-trained to inpaint upon randomly masked sequences. Therefore, scaling pre-training directly improves asynchronous performance, and the native forward pass suits inference-time inpainting;

Fine-tuning Free. As a consequence, inpainting-specific patterns are implicitly introduced during pre-training, making discrete diffusion a fine-tuning-free approach for high-quality, out-of-the-box asynchronous execution;

Natural Guidance. Moreover, with discrete diffusion policies, we can early-exit inference once the necessary action tokens are unmasked, leaving the remaining masking pattern as an adaptive and natural guidance for the next inference;

Lower Inference Cost. Finally, with committed tokens from previous chunks, the tokens to unmask per inference is reduced, leading to lower inference cost for inpainting.

We propose the resulting async execution method, DiscreteRTC, which replaces the external correctiosn in flow-matching-based RTC with the native unmasking operation of discrete diffusion policies.
Experiments on the dynamic simulated benchmark Kinetix [27] and real-world dynamic manipulation demonstrate that DiscreteRTC achieves higher success rates than flow-matching-based RTC and other baselines.
In summary, DiscreteRTC is simpler to implement with 0 lines of code for async inpainting, faster at inference with only  0.7×\ 0.7\times computation compared with generating actions from scratch while flow-matching-based RTC requires ∼1.7×\sim 1.7\times computation, and better at execution with 50%\% higher success rate in real-world dynamic pick over flow-matching-based RTC.

